  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 111 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nLate in the week and through this weekend, an upper level trough \nwill dig across the eastern US and enhance across most of the \nEastern Seaboard before settling off the New England coast in the \nwestern Atlantic. A frontal boundary will push southwards in tandem \nwith the trough strengthening and the front will eventually settle \nover far southern Florida or the Florida Straits this weekend. As \nthe front stalls, much drier air will advect into Central Florida, \nbut most of this will not make it into South Florida. However, PWATs \nwill still fall to average or below average levels with the lake \nregion seeing the lowest values. With this anticipated less moisture \navailable, shower and thunderstorm coverage is expected to decrease \nprimarily for the lake region. This is reflected with PoPs of 25-40% \nthis weekend near the lake and 50-70% for the southern half of the \nforecast area. Despite the decrease in PoPs for a lot of the area, \nthe pattern will still remain active and unsettled through the \nforecast period. \n\nDaily high temperatures each day will continue to reach the upper \n80s to near 90 with overnight lows mostly in the low to mid 70s. \nHowever, areas near the lake could see temperatures drop into the \nupper 60s several nights.",
